braam — the commands
====================

A command word is a shell function first, then one of the builtins below, then
a file that PATH names — /bin:/pkg/bin at boot. Only the last of the three
costs a process: a program is a wasm binary in a worker of its own, with its
own address space, its own memory cap and a kill switch behind ^C.

A builtin lives inside /bin/sh, either because it touches the shell's own
state — its working directory, its variables, its jobs — or because its whole
cost would have been the spawn. echo, test, true and false are of the second
kind, so each is a builtin *and* a file in /bin: the builtin is what a prompt
runs, the file is what anything spawning the name by itself gets. [ and : are
of that kind too and have no file, being punctuation nothing spawns.

This list is what the boot archive ships, and is a file: /share/help. A
program installed somewhere else on PATH runs perfectly well and is not named
here. Each program also answers for itself — a usage error prints `usage: …`
and exits 2.

Shell builtins
--------------

  .         <file> — run a file's commands in this shell
  :         [<arg>...] — do nothing, successfully
  [         <expr> ] — evaluate a condition, as `test` does
  break     [<n>] — leave a loop, or n of them
  cd        [<dir>] — change the working directory, /home by default
  command   -v <name>... — say what a command word would run
  continue  [<n>] — start a loop's next turn
  echo      [-n] [<word>...] — write the arguments
  eval      [<arg>...] — join the arguments and run them as a command
  exec      [<command>] — keep its redirections, or run it and leave
  exit      [<status>] — end the shell
  export    [<name>[=<value>]...] — put a variable in each child's environment
  false     fail
  fg        [%n] — wait for a background job in the foreground
  jobs      list the jobs started with &
  kill      %n — cancel a job
  read      <name>... — one line of input, split across the names
  readonly  [<name>[=<value>]...] — refuse further assignment to a variable
  return    [<status>] — leave a function or a sourced file
  set       [-eux] [--] [<arg>...] — the options, the parameters, or a listing
  shift     [<n>] — drop the first n positional parameters
  test      <expr> — evaluate a condition and report it as a status
  trap      [<action>|-] 0|2 — run an action when the shell ends, or on ^C
  true      succeed
  unset     [-f] <name>... — remove a variable, or a function with -f
  wait      [%n...] — wait for a background job, or for all of them

Programs in /bin
----------------

  cat       [<file>...] — copy files, or the input, to the output
  chat      <url> [<nick>] — talk over a WebSocket to whoever else is there
  clear     blank the screen
  curl      [-i] [-X <method>] [-H <header>] [-d <data>] <url> — fetch a URL
  date      [-u] — print the date and time
  df        [-h] — report filesystem space, and with -h in scaled units
  echo      [-n] [<word>...] — write the arguments
  edit      <file> — a full-screen editor; ^S saves, ^Q quits
  env       [-i] [<name>=<value>...] [<command> [<arg>...]]
            — print the environment, or run a command with it changed
  false     fail
  fexport   <file> [<name>] — download a file out of the browser
  fimport   [<dir>] — copy files from the browser into /import
  grep      [-i] [-v] <text> [<file>...] — pass matching lines
  head      [-n <count>] [<file>...] — the first lines, ten by default
  help      page this list, which is /share/help
  hog       take memory until the cap refuses more
  less      [<file>] — page a file on a terminal, copy it off one; q quits
  ln        -s [-f] <target> <name> — make a symbolic link
  ls        [-1CRSdhlrt] [<path>...] — list directories, in columns on a
            terminal
  mkdir     [-p] <dir>... — create directories, with missing parents under -p
  mount     list the mounted filesystems
  mv        [-fi] <src>... <dst> — move or rename files
  pbcopy    [<file>...] — put the input on the clipboard
  pbpaste   write the clipboard to the output
  pkg       <command> [<arg>...] — install and remove packages
  ps        list the tasks the kernel is running, and which are processes
  pwd       print the working directory
  rm        [-r] <path>... — remove files, or directories with -r
  sh        [-eux] [-s | -c <command> | <file>] [<arg>...] — the shell
  sleep     [-m] <secs> — wait, in seconds, or milliseconds with -m
  spin      [<millions>] — loop without yielding, to be killed
  tail      [-n <count>] [<file>...] — the last lines, ten by default
  test      <expr> — evaluate a condition and report it as a status
  timeout   [-m] <secs> <command> [<arg>...]
            — run a command, killing it after a delay (124)
  touch     <file>... — create empty files, or move an existing one's mtime
  true      succeed
  uname     [-a|-s|-r|-m] — name the system, and with -a the host it runs on
  vmstat    [-s] [-m] [<secs> [<count>]] — what the kernel is doing, as rates
  watch     [-m] [-n <secs>] <command> [<arg>...] — run a command repeatedly
  wc        [<file>...] — count lines, words and bytes

Packages
--------

  `pkg` installs into /pkg, which the boot archive does not carry — so what it
  installed outlives a release that replaces /bin and /share. /pkg/bin is on
  PATH after /bin, so nothing installed can shadow a system command.

  Nothing is installed unchecked. The repository's index is signed, the keys
  it is checked against are in /share/pkg/anchor and come out of the boot
  archive, and every package is compared against the size and the digest that
  index gave before a byte of it is unpacked. A check that fails names the
  step it failed at, and nothing is half-installed. Its commands, each of them
  spelled `pkg <name>`:

  update    fetch the repository index, and check it before storing it
  search    <pattern> — search the index by name and description
  info      <package> — what the index says about one package
  install   <package>... — install packages, and what they depend on
  remove    <package>... — remove packages
  autoremove
            remove dependencies nothing explicit needs any more
  upgrade   upgrade everything installed
  list      list installed packages
  files     <package> — the files an installed package placed
  verify    [<package>] — re-read those files, and compare each against the
            digest recorded when it was installed, and report a package whose
            install script failed
  clean     drop the downloaded archives, and what no generation still needs.
            The generation before the live one is kept

  An install builds the whole new generation under /pkg/gen and then renames
  one symbolic link, /pkg/active, over the old one. That rename is the moment
  it happens: before it nothing has changed, and after it everything has. The
  generation `clean` keeps is the one before, and going back to it is putting
  the link back yourself — `ln -sf /pkg/gen/<n> /pkg/active`. There is no
  `pkg rollback`.

  `pkg verify` says a file changed since its install. It is not a guarantee
  that none did. Checking happens once, at install; nothing re-reads the store
  afterwards, there are no file permissions here, and anything may overwrite
  anything — /bin/pkg included.

More
----

  /README           what this system is, in a page
  /share/motd       what boot prints
  /share/pkg/anchor the keys a repository's index is checked against
  /proc             the system describing itself as text — try cat /proc/host

  help | grep ls    off a terminal `less` copies, so this list pipes
